# §B. Checklist R1–R9

**R1 — PASS.** h = r/2 = 1/40; h/L = (1/40)/24 = 1/960 exactly; z = e^{i2π/960}
= e^{iπ/480}; gcd(1,960) = 1 makes z a primitive 960th root of unity. Order
960; z ∉ {0, 1, −1}.

**R2 — PASS, derived from scratch.** For a stationary Gaussian field with
spectral representation Var(Λf) = Σ ρ_ξ |S_Λ(ξ)|², the symbol of evaluation at
x_± = (±h, 0) applied to e^{iξ·x} is e^{±iξ₁h} = z^{±m}, and ∂_x, ∂_y insert
factors X = i2πm/L, Y = i2πn/L. Hence the six endpoint functionals contribute
exactly z^{−m}A_−(X,Y) + z^{m}A_+(X,Y) with A_± ∈ span{1, X, Y}. Because both
endpoints sit **on the x-axis**, no z-type phase in n appears — this is what
makes an m-only residue argument sufficient (§C2).

**R3 — PASS, with its basis stated.** Midpoint jets ∂^{(j,k)}f(0) have symbols
X^jY^k with no phase; the nine declared multi-indices give the nine **distinct**
monomials Y², X²Y, XY², Y³, X⁴, X³Y, X²Y², XY³, Y⁴. The crosswalk to
(q,a,w,z,c₄₀…c₀₄) as nonzero scalings/signs of pure derivative functionals
matches the convention of the **terminally closed** GP-CLS-006/008 packages,
whose covariance content I previously verified by independent recomputation
(AO48-AUD-021 §3, double-factorial rule, all entries). Nonzero diagonal
rescaling preserves independence. GP-AUD-163's failure mode D (a hidden
lower-order admixture) does not arise under the pure-jet convention those
closed packages fix.

**R4 — PASS, premise re-derived rather than cited.** The periodized
Bargmann–Fock covariance is the lattice wrapping of the plane covariance
e^{−|x|²/2}, whose spectral density ∝ e^{−|ξ|²/2} is a Gaussian — **strictly
positive everywhere, with no zeros to sample**. The torus weights are its
values at ξ_{m,n}, hence ρ_{m,n} > 0 at *every* lattice point; normalization
rescales by a positive constant. Therefore Var = 0 ⟺ the symbol vanishes on
all of Z². Control C5's caveat is correctly identified as load-bearing and is
discharged by the Gaussian's zero-free density.

**R5 — PASS.** On m = c + 960k: z^m = z^c, so the vanishing symbol becomes
P_c(X(k), Y(n)) = 0 for all (k,n) ∈ Z², where P_c = z^{−c}A_− + A_0 + z^cA_+
and X(k), Y(n) are affine with nonzero slopes (i·2π·960/L and i·2π/L). A
two-variable polynomial vanishing on Z² is identically zero (fix n: infinitely
many k-roots; then each k-coefficient, a polynomial in n, has infinitely many
roots). Invertibility of the affine substitution over ℂ transfers this to the
polynomial identity P_c ≡ 0 in (X,Y), for c = 0, 1, 2. Exact; no density or
limiting step; the restriction of X to a residue class is harmless precisely
because the composed polynomial is killed first (GP-AUD-163's mode F,
independently confirmed).

**R6 — PASS, by a route not in the source.** M(z) is the **Vandermonde matrix**
of the nodes (z⁻¹, 1, z): row i, column j is w_j^{i−1}. Hence

det M = (1 − z⁻¹)(z − z⁻¹)(z − 1) = (z−1)³(z+1)/z²,

expanding (1−z⁻¹) = (z−1)/z and (z−z⁻¹) = (z−1)(z+1)/z. An independent
cofactor expansion gives z² − 2z + 2z⁻¹ − z⁻², and (z−1)³(z+1) = z⁴−2z³+2z−1;
dividing by z² matches term-for-term. Nonzero ⟺ z ∉ {0, 1, −1} — exactly the
Vandermonde distinct-nodes condition, satisfied at the primitive 960th root.
The Vandermonde view also makes the mutation controls transparent: z = 1
collapses all three nodes; z = −1 collapses nodes 1 and 3.

**R7 — PASS.** A_± ≡ 0 in the basis {1, X, Y} kills the six endpoint
coefficients; A_0 ≡ 0 in nine distinct monomials kills the nine midpoint
coefficients. Fifteen of fifteen. The argument runs over ℂ, which is stronger
than the real-combination statement needed.

**R8 — PASS.** G_joint ≻ 0 ⟹ the principal pin block G_pin ≻ 0 ⟹ the Schur
complement Σ = S − C G_pin⁻¹Cᵀ ≻ 0: for v ≠ 0, the joint vector
(−G_pin⁻¹Cᵀv, v) has quadratic form vᵀΣv > 0. A nondegenerate 9-dim Gaussian
has a continuous strictly positive density, so every positive-width subbox has
positive mass. Note the conditioning *values* (b, b − r³/6) enter means only —
GP-DER-161 §2's remark is correct and worth keeping.

**R9 — PASS.** No finite-Q4 substitution (all functionals are exact-field
jets); no capture, probability-magnitude, r-uniformity, or promotion claim
imported; positivity is proved, never assumed via a vanishing weight; the
phase-reversal convention check (GP-AUD-163 mode A) holds trivially in the
Vandermonde picture — reversing z ↔ z⁻¹ permutes the nodes, and a Vandermonde
determinant is nonzero under any ordering of distinct nodes.

# §B5. Two observations — the proof has slack it never spends

1. **The degree structure is unused.** A_± have degree ≤ 1 and A_0 degree ≥ 2,
   with disjoint monomial supports — so even a *two*-residue-class argument
   plus the disjointness would finish. GP-DER-161 uses three classes and never
   touches degrees, which means the proof survives conventions in which the
   midpoint coordinates acquire lower-order admixtures, as long as the fifteen
   functionals' combined monomial list stays independent per support. That is
   the right way around: the robustness covers exactly the failure mode
   (GP-AUD-163's D) that a leaner proof would be exposed to.
2. **The exact route explains the numerical wall it replaces.** At the true
   phase, |det M| = |z−1|³|z+1| ≈ 5.6 × 10⁻⁷ (§B6). The separation is real but
   *tiny* — three sample points 1/960th of a period apart — which is precisely
   why CL-AUD-081's double-precision eigensolve hit an unresolvable minimum
   eigenvalue. The ill-conditioning CL reported and the exact nondegeneracy
   proved here are the same geometry seen by two instruments; the artifacts
   corroborate rather than contradict each other, exactly as GP-DER-161 §8
   states.

# §B6. Machine receipt (exact, stdlib only, this session)

Integer-coefficient Laurent-polynomial arithmetic, no floating point in the
identity step: det(M)·z² expands to exactly {z⁴: +1, z³: −2, z¹: +2, z⁰: −1},
and (z−1)³(z+1) expands to the identical coefficient dictionary — **IDENTITY
HOLDS as a polynomial identity in ℤ[z]**, hence at every nonzero z, no
truncation, no epsilon. Mutation controls evaluated exactly: det(1) = 0,
det(−1) = 0. Order check: 1/960 in lowest terms, order 960. Corroborating
double-precision evaluation at z = e^{iπ/480}: cofactor determinant, closed
formula, and my Vandermonde product agree pairwise to ≲ 4.5 × 10⁻¹⁹, with
common magnitude 5.607 × 10⁻⁷ — nonzero and equal to |z−1|³|z+1|.

